From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: uianimation: Windows Animation animates

Wine's Windows Animation was stubs: storyboards did nothing, transitions
could not be created, the timer never ticked. Paint.NET animates its
controls with it and died on the first CreateSmoothStopTransition.

Now variables follow transitions laid out on storyboards (one after another
per variable, or at keyframes; between keyframes stretched to fit), and
the manager's Update evaluates every playing storyboard at the time it is
given and calls the variables' change handlers (after dropping its lock),
the storyboards' and its own. The transition library's transitions are
all there (instantaneous, constant, discrete, linear and from speed,
sinusoidal from velocity and from range, accelerate-decelerate, reversal,
cubic, smooth stop, parabolic) and the transition factory wraps a
program's interpolator. A newer storyboard takes a variable over from an
older one. The timer tells the time and, enabled, ticks its client (the
manager is one) on the enabling thread's message loop while the client is
busy. Priority comparisons, repeats and longest acceptable delays are
accepted and not acted on.

Gate: test/d2dfx-gate.sh. uianimation:uianimation 0 failures, todo_wine
removed (the timer's states, transitions, E_POINTER).
